Application Component Composition
=================================

This CML requirement model defines how Textus CBD Support helps an application
designer plan an application from attributable existing CAR and SAR evidence
and explicitly identified component gaps.

# VISION

## EvidenceBackedApplicationComposition

### SUMMARY

Make an intended Textus application explainable as a composition of existing
components, unresolved requirements, and proposed component responsibilities.

### GOAL

Enable a human developer or an MCP-connected generative-AI client to describe
an application goal and receive a reviewable component composition whose
existing-component claims are grounded in attributable CAR and SAR evidence.

### PRECONDITION

Textus treats component selection and application composition as explicit
modeling concerns rather than as an unrecorded search or prompt result.

### POSTCONDITION

Application composition can be discussed through shared intent, capability,
coverage, gap, proposal, evidence, and decision vocabulary.

# CONTEXT

## ApplicationCompositionBackground

### SUMMARY

Describe the information needed to plan an application from reusable
components.

### DESCRIPTION

An application description may contain actors, stakeholder goals, desired
behaviors, data responsibilities, external integrations, quality expectations,
runtime constraints, deployment assumptions, and implementation preferences.

CBD Support currently observes component identity, kind, source, version,
freshness, runtime compatibility, operations, references, artifacts,
dependencies, conflicts, and explicit evidence absences.  Application
composition additionally requires structured statements about the capabilities
provided and required by each component and about the application requirements
that those capabilities cover.

# SYSTEM CONTEXT

## CbdSupportCompositionBoundary

### SUMMARY

Define the boundary between application-composition support and component
generation or execution.

### DESCRIPTION

CBD Support collects and reconciles component evidence, accounts for
application-requirement coverage, preserves conflicts and limitations, and
returns a composition plan.  Semantic or AI providers may interpret intent or
propose component boundaries through attributable provider contracts.

Cozy and CNCF remain responsible for generating, assembling, building, and
running CAR and SAR artifacts.  A composition plan does not claim that an
application has been generated or executed.

# CAPABILITY

## ApplicationIntentCapture

### SUMMARY

Preserve what the application designer wants to build before interpretation.

### GOAL

Retain a stable application-intent identity, the original description, actors,
stakeholder goals, desired behavior, data and integration responsibilities,
quality expectations, runtime and deployment constraints, preferences, and
unresolved questions.

### PRECONDITION

The application designer supplies an application purpose and at least one
desired behavior.

### POSTCONDITION

The original statements remain distinguishable from deterministic, model, and
human interpretations derived from them.

## RequiredCapabilityModeling

### SUMMARY

Represent one bounded capability required by the admitted application intent.

### GOAL

Give every functional, quality, integration, data, runtime, or operational
capability a stable identity, originating intent statement, mandatory or
optional status, acceptance condition, semantic terms, assumptions,
confidence, and interpretation provenance.

### PRECONDITION

One admitted application intent is available for decomposition.

### POSTCONDITION

The application has a bounded capability inventory that can be compared with
component evidence.

## ComponentCapabilityEvidence

### SUMMARY

Describe what one observed component can provide without completing missing
facts from another source.

### GOAL

Relate exact component, source, kind, and version identity to provided and
required capabilities, realizing services and operations, dependencies,
runtime and deployment constraints, quality evidence, supported use cases,
known limitations, freshness, and diagnostics.

### PRECONDITION

An attributable component observation is available.

### POSTCONDITION

Each component-capability statement identifies whether it is an observed fact,
deterministic inference, model inference, or human decision and retains the
supporting Evidence.

## ApplicationCapabilityCoverage

### SUMMARY

Account for how existing components cover the application's required
capabilities.

### GOAL

Bind each required capability to one selected component, a bounded alternative
set, a composition gap, or an explicit unresolved result.

### PRECONDITION

The required-capability inventory and relevant component evidence are
available.

### POSTCONDITION

Coverage status, rationale, supporting and missing evidence, conflicts,
constraints, and selection-decision provenance remain reviewable.

## MissingComponentProposal

### SUMMARY

Describe a candidate component responsibility for application capabilities
not covered by existing evidence.

### GOAL

Propose a provisional component identity, kind, responsibility boundary,
provided and required capabilities, services and operations, dependencies,
runtime and quality constraints, rationale, alternatives, confidence, open
questions, provider identity, and human confirmation state.

### PRECONDITION

One or more required capabilities remain uncovered after existing-component
analysis.

### POSTCONDITION

The proposed component remains a design artifact distinct from every existing
catalog component until a human decision promotes it into component modeling.

## ApplicationCompositionPlanning

### SUMMARY

Produce the reviewable result that combines intent, coverage, existing
components, gaps, and proposed components.

### GOAL

Return selected existing components, bounded alternatives,
requirement-to-component coverage, dependency and runtime composition,
composition gaps, proposed components, conflicts, limitations, assumptions,
open questions, Evidence provenance, inference provenance, and human
decisions.

### PRECONDITION

Application intent, required capabilities, and available component evidence
have been admitted.

### POSTCONDITION

Every admitted application requirement has an explicit disposition and every
material statement has attributable evidence or decision provenance.

# CONSTRAINT

## EvidenceAndInferenceSeparation

### SUMMARY

Existing-component facts cite attributable source evidence.  Deterministic
inference, model inference, and human decisions remain explicitly labeled and
do not alter the cited observation.

## TotalRequirementAccounting

### SUMMARY

Every admitted required capability appears in the composition result as
selected, alternative, proposed, or unresolved.

## NoHiddenWinner

### SUMMARY

Source priority, match score, or model preference does not silently select one
component when the admitted evidence remains ambiguous or conflicting.

## ProposalIsNotCatalogFact

### SUMMARY

A proposed component remains a separate design artifact until an explicit
human decision promotes it into a component-development workflow.

## AssemblyIsNotExecution

### SUMMARY

The composition plan supplies information needed to assemble an application;
it does not claim that a SAR has been generated, built, or run.

# USE CASE

## plan_application_component_composition

### ID

UC-CBD-001

### SUMMARY

Describe an application to build and obtain an evidence-backed composition
plan containing the required existing components and proposed new components
for responsibilities not covered by available evidence.

### ACTOR

A human developer or an MCP-connected generative-AI client designing a Textus
application.

### PRIMARY ACTOR

ApplicationDesigner

### SUPPORTING ACTOR

CatalogProvider, SemanticKnowledgeProvider, CompositionAdvisor

### STAKEHOLDER

ApplicationOwner

### GOAL

Determine how the intended application can be realized from existing CAR and
SAR components, make every uncovered responsibility explicit, and propose
bounded component responsibilities for the remaining work.

### TRIGGER

The application designer describes an application that they want to create.

### PRIORITY

high

### STATUS

draft

### PRECONDITION

The application designer supplies an application purpose and at least one
required behavior.  CBD Support has access to attributable component evidence
or can report explicitly that the required evidence is unavailable.

### POSTCONDITION

Every admitted application requirement is covered by one selected existing
component, associated with bounded alternatives, assigned to a proposed new
component responsibility, or retained as unresolved because the evidence is
insufficient or conflicting.

The resulting plan retains the source, version, freshness, runtime,
dependency, conflict, limitation, assumption, and inference provenance needed
to review each disposition.

### MAIN FLOW

#### evidence_backed_application_composition

1. The application designer supplies the application purpose, actors, desired
   behaviors, data responsibilities, external integrations, quality
   expectations, runtime constraints, deployment assumptions, and optional
   implementation preferences.
2. CBD Support retains the original intent and identifies missing decision
   inputs as open questions.
3. The intent is decomposed into bounded required capabilities, each retaining
   its originating statement and interpretation provenance.
4. CBD Support searches attributable catalog, development, local artifact,
   cache, and semantic evidence for relevant CAR and SAR components.
5. CBD Support returns source-preserving candidates with identity, kind,
   version, freshness, runtime compatibility, operations, dependencies,
   limitations, and Evidence.
6. CBD Support evaluates how the candidates collectively cover the required
   capabilities and records overlaps, gaps, dependency conflicts, runtime
   incompatibilities, missing evidence, and assumptions.
7. A composition advisor groups uncovered responsibilities into proposed
   component boundaries with explicit rationale, confidence, provider
   identity, and open questions.
8. CBD Support presents the selected existing components, bounded alternatives,
   capability coverage, composition graph, gaps, proposed components,
   conflicts, limitations, assumptions, and provenance as one reviewable plan.

#### explicit_component_alternatives

1. Two or more existing components plausibly cover the same required
   capability.
2. CBD Support preserves every bounded alternative with its source, version,
   match rationale, constraints, and supporting Evidence.
3. No component becomes selected until an admitted selection policy or human
   decision chooses it.

#### insufficient_component_evidence

1. A required capability has a possible component match whose capability,
   runtime, dependency, or freshness evidence is insufficient.
2. CBD Support records the candidate and the missing evidence without claiming
   coverage.
3. The composition result keeps the capability unresolved or assigns it to a
   provisional component proposal with the insufficiency visible.

#### no_existing_component

1. No attributable existing component covers one or more required
   capabilities.
2. CBD Support records a composition gap and the considered evidence.
3. A composition advisor proposes a bounded new component responsibility.
4. The proposal remains separate from catalog facts and awaits human decision.

#### incompatible_composition

1. Individually relevant components have incompatible dependency versions,
   runtime constraints, or composition assumptions.
2. CBD Support preserves the conflict paths and affected requirements.
3. The composition plan presents alternatives or an unresolved result instead
   of silently choosing a winner.
